> > Does anyone have any other suggestions for monsters to
> > be found in a corrupted prison?
>
> Rogue and convict player-monsters are the first two things that
> spring to mind.

Used sparingly, sure.

> Bats seem atmospheric, and maybe snakes and worms.

Bats in a prison seems a little cheesy to me, and they're pretty
much everywhere in NetHack already.  I'm not sure I see the
point of including them.

> All the s and S monsters seem like the right kind of vermin.

I can see that.  Well, mostly; I'm not so sure about cobras
specifically, but apart from that...

> wouldn't mind seeing ghosts, shades, and potentially even
> skeletons (I think they might be a little high in terms of
> difficulty, though).

Pending difficulty balancing, I think I can agree with this.
Ghosts particularly would seem to fit -- not all undead generally,
though.  Mummies and zombies and liches don't really belong.

> Iron golems would be nice, if only in that they drop iron
> chains when killed and there really ought to be a bunch
> of iron chains littering the floors of a jail.

I agree.  None of the other golems would really fit, but the iron
golems make sense, what with all the bars and chains and so forth
typically associated with prisons (especially the "traditional"
sort of prison you'd expect to see in a medieval/fantasy context
or in the third world -- the kind of prison where the inmates get
worse medical care than the guards and may not get three square
meals all 365 days a year).

> Have you considered using Keystone Kops as de facto law
> enforcement monsters?  They may not be tough enough for a
> quest-ready character...

Kops by themselves are wimpy in the extreme, but the fact that
their pies can blind you could be interesting in combination
with monsters that don't show up on telepathy, especially if
there were some of the latter that could move pretty quickly
(though I can't think of anything in that category, particularly
within the constraints of a prison setting).  I assume a quest
hero will have telepathy, so anything visible to that will not
be helped much by blinding; and things that aren't visible to
telepathy but are really slow (e.g., ghosts) won't have a chance
to do much before the player can kill the Kops and recover vision.

On the whole, I think it would be good to include some soldiers.
Throwing in some Kops too would be okay for flavor, but if you want
the Kops to contribute much to difficulty you need to pair them
with something mindless and relatively fast -- gas spores, perhaps,
or air elementals, but I can't think of a particularly compelling
storyline reason for either of those things to be in a prison.


